.title = "Teleprompter: Learning with Zig?",
.date = .date("2026-08-29"),
.author = "anirudh chhangani",
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = true,
---

Started learning zig!. Why?, why a new language in the day and age of LLM's, are you mad? Let AI do the work.

Because I want to.

The first real programming language I learned was C in my college days. Programming was hard, harder than I had expected it to be.
I did a lot of html and css before college, but never a real programming language. Not even python/php etc.
And starting with C made it that much harder. Over the course of my semester while learning C, every problem I solved gave me a sense of confidence,
 which was broken the next problem I solved. But at the end of it all that endurance, it made me understand computers, on what goes in it.

After the first semester, we moved to java, and man was it frustrating, why do we need to learn a language when we barely learnt C.
I became a rebel, asked professors why java, what does it bring to the table, why do we need to learn it over C and none could answer this curiosity of mine.

Ultimately we moved on from java to web technologies of the time, THE LAMP stack, python, perl, etc.

I joined the industry after my degree was over, I had learnt django in one of my internships and django was all the rage in our startup ecosystem and hence
Python was THE language that I ended up using in the industry and continue to use today even after 10 years of being in the industry.

But, there's a catch, ah there's always a catch. In these 10 years I never did have to use the C language, my infatuation and personal experiments never stopped
on finding the answer to my question, why not C?

I came across a lot of those answers and every one of them sounded really solid and reasonable to me on why industry did not prefer C.

* Memory unsafe.
* Manual memory management reduces productivity.
* Large codebases are hard to maintain with C.
* Implicit behaviours, undefined behaviours.
* etc.

And these reasons sounded so perfect on why not C, that whenever I tried learning C, I always found it hard to get started and could resonate with
these reasons. But, yeah there's a but again. I always wondered, C being such a simple language, why do we need a complex languages? And why were there no
major efforts to improve on C? Yeah there were a lot of efforts over the years, but nothing panned out really, what we got instead was C++, Java and with it all the complexity.

C lacked all the modern features that we take for granted in languages like python which make development faster and easier.

* Package Management.
* Modern tooling.
* etc.

Yeah, and somehow I stumbled upon Zig, A better C, not just better, but a better alternative. From what it looks like at the moment anyways,
I have written basic programs in zig anyways, what do I know.

BUT why zig? why not just use LLM, C3, or any other language?

Because I want to.

Anyways these were just a bunch of thoughts I had while I was trying to learn Zig.
